Reason from missing replies without inventing remote observations (@computing-channel-failures: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Specify-one-outstanding-operation-and-a-retained-durable-result.lingua#r_00529B7EA6239E5D1C8E61D93C") { r_008C1CDFEA1BACFF3D8BA60B87
Declare the failure model before choosing retries. Our message channel can delay, reorder, drop and duplicate whole messages. There is no bound on delay or duplicate lifetime. Messages are authentic and their contents remain unchanged. Endpoints may crash and recover between modeled atomic durable actions. They do not fabricate replies, lose durable state or restart with reused operation identities. A network partition can prevent delivery indefinitely unless a later progress premise excludes that execution. These are assumptions to test when transferring the construction, not properties of every network.

Consider a client that sent request (1,2), waited and has no result. In history A the request was dropped before reaching the server. In history B the request changed the server's account from zero to two but the reply was dropped. In history C the request is still delayed. In history D the server received it but has not committed it. The client's local record can be identical in all four histories. Any deterministic decision based only on that record must be identical too, even though the remote account is different. This is an indistinguishability argument, not a claim that every distributed decision is impossible.

A timeout is a local decision point that may trigger a retry or report uncertainty. In this model it is not proof that the server failed or that an effect did not happen. A slow but successful request can outlive any finite timeout when delay has no finite upper bound. Ending the wait and returning an unknown outcome is different from rolling back a remote effect. A later cancellation message is another request that can itself be delayed or lost; cancellation semantics require a separate protocol.

Retain the exact outstanding identity and arguments while retrying. Giving the same intended operation a fresh identity can legitimately ask the service to perform another operation. Conversely, attaching the old identity to different arguments makes duplicate detection ambiguous. Our receiver will compare both identity and amount for its retained last result. It will reject a changed amount under the same identity. Field validation establishes syntactic admissibility; it cannot infer that the caller selected the correct real-world amount.

An accidental-corruption check is not part of our channel. To see why a simple sum cannot establish unchanged content, compare two byte pairs [1,4] and [2,3]: both have sum five. Even a check that detects a particular accidental error does not, by that fact alone, authenticate a sender. If corruption or forgery is admitted, add explicit validation and security contracts and revisit the proof. We do not silently interpret malformed or forged messages as ordinary loss.

Our original explorer represents each direction by a set of message values. Delivering a member may leave it available for another delivery; dropping removes it. This deliberately admits arbitrarily many duplicate deliveries while keeping finitely many distinct values. It does not count physical copies, buffer capacities, packet timings or probabilities. Every finite exploration trace can be realized by producing enough identical copies before the modeled drops. Claims about finite buffer occupancy or traffic volume cannot be inferred from this representation.

**Read and work:** compare [Remote Procedure Call Protocol Specification Version 2 RFC 5531](../sources/Remote-Procedure-Call-Protocol-Specification-Version-2-RFC-5531.lingua#r_0058E17EDA89658DCF6713FD72) section 5's transport and execution distinctions with the four histories above. Reuse [Separate safety from progress and account for waiting](Separate-safety-from-progress-and-account-for-waiting.lingua#r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8) to separate a safety claim from an eventual-delivery claim. Write the client's identical local observations for A and B and the server's different states. Explain why retrying can recover a result without proving that the first message was lost. State exactly which premise must change to admit an unauthenticated endpoint, two simultaneous clients or loss of durable identity state.

**Continue:** [Specify one outstanding operation and a retained durable result](Specify-one-outstanding-operation-and-a-retained-durable-result.lingua#r_00529B7EA6239E5D1C8E61D93C).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
